.TH MACHO\-O2A 1 "October 3, 2026" "MachO-Tools 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-o2a \- map a file offset to a virtual address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-o2a
.I offset
.RI [ offset ...]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports which virtual address each given file offset corresponds to, together with
the section it falls in and the slice it belongs to. The inverse of
.BR macho\-a2o (1).
.PP
Offsets are absolute positions in the file, accepted as decimal or as hex with a
.B 0x
prefix. The prefix is required for hex so that
.B 10
is never silently read as
.BR 0x10 .
.SH SLICES
A
.B section_64
structure's
.B offset
is relative to the start of its
.IR slice ,
so on a universal binary a relative offset and a position in the file are different
numbers. In
.IR /bin/ls ,
whose x86_64 slice begins at 0x4000 and whose arm64 slice begins at 0x10000, the same
relative offset names different bytes in each.
.PP
Every slice is examined and each answer attributed to the one that produced it,
because the same file offset is a different address in each. Where more than one
slice maps an offset, that is reported rather than resolved.
.SH OFFSETS THAT MAP TO NOTHING
The Mach-O header and the load commands lie inside
.BR __TEXT 's
segment range but in no section, so they resolve to a real address with no section
name. That is an answer, not a gap. Past the last mapped byte, nothing maps at all,
and that is a negative result.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Read one architecture,
.B x86_64
or
.BR arm64 .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read. Every positional argument is an offset, so the binary cannot be
taken from the last one.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings: a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive a JSON number.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one offset resolved to an address.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and no offset mapped. A negative result, not a failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including an offset that is neither decimal nor
.BR 0x -prefixed hex.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read or parsed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-o2a 0x1000 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go
macho\-o2a \-\-json 0x1000 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq '.data.queries[0].vaddr'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Follow a literal's file offset from
.BR macho\-findliteral (1)
to the address a symbol lookup needs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-o2a \-\-json 0x59d140 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq -r '.data.queries[0].vaddr'
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Every positional argument is an offset, so the binary comes from
.B \-b
rather than from the last positional, for the same reason
.BR macho\-symlookup (1)
does it that way.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-a2o (1),
.BR macho\-findliteral (1),
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1),
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR otool (1),
.BR nm (1)